Z||000000||FieroLisa||12-25-2000||08:43 PM||||I'm doing something with the 86 GT in terms of exhaust, I just don't know what yet. What I'm looking for is people's opinions on different exhaust systems, or tips, and also pros and cons of leaving the cat off.<BR>I won't have to pass emissions check so that's not a concern.<BR>Any pics posted would be appreciated, if you suggest something and know the price of it, and the work included in installing it, let me know that too.<BR>Thanks a lot!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>86 gold 5 speed SC (For Sale)<BR>Columbus, OH/Greensboro NC<BR>*Lots of New Pics!* <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/</A>||||reg||6
Z||000001||polomeno||12-25-2000||09:02 PM||||Get some good headers.. FOCOA has great ones, and i think fieroshop has the borla exhaust .. very nice.. do a search and you'll see some info on it<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||12-25-2000||09:12 PM||||Lisa, if it's a stock V6 the best bang for the buck is to port the stock manifolds and a free flow cat. <P>Now if want a ton of info on pros & cons<BR>do search: Mufflers, Exhaust, Headers, Tips,<BR>Manifolds, Flow Master any of these will work.<BR>Be ready it's alot!!!!<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||1
Z||000003||Black88GT||12-25-2000||10:25 PM||||I have a Borla exhaust, ported manifolds, and no cat. on my 88 GT.  I can definitely say I felt a difference on the highway after the exhaust was put on and cat. removed.<BR>It sounds nice too.  I would recommend getting T-304 stainless for the cat. eliminator pipe, Rodney dickman sells it.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000004||BAM-BAM||12-25-2000||10:36 PM|||| Lisa, Look in the mall for the two Borlas that are still for sale from FieroParts.com. I just bought one that will be installed with the 3.4L HO this spring. It's an awesome deal, well under $400.00.<P> Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach/KYB Susp.<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats,headliner! MOMO Commando Steering Wheel, White Face Race Style Gauges And Soon To Be More!!! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||12-25-2000||11:34 PM||||The usual reminder/warning...... Removal of emissions devices, in this case the catalyst, is a federal crime. It varies by state how this is enforced. You likely won't get arrested but you may not pass inspection. Just because you don't have an emisions test today don't think you won't ever have one. The feds are hell bent on forcing all the contig 48 states to accept emisions testing of some kind. Pending legislation ties big money to this. Don't do it and lose the cash.<P>Start with "simple" things. like cleaning up the exhaust manifolds. A port job would be next. Even basic porting can help tremendously. <P>If the catalyst is beat replace it with a high flow job.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Shaft||12-26-2000||12:36 AM||||Didn't you say you were on vacation in Hawaii?? What are you doing on here?? Go out on the beach and enjoy the warm weather while you can. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroLisa||12-26-2000||01:35 AM||||As for emissions, the reason I don't have to pass it now is because in Ohio the Fiero is considered a collectors car and those are not require for emissions testing since they are not your primary car. <P>Shaft:  Well its gettin late here, so its OK that I'm online and gotta keep in contact with people somehow, phone bills get a wee bit expensive from here  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>86 gold 5 speed SC (For Sale)<BR>Columbus, OH/Greensboro NC<BR>*Lots of New Pics!* <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||12-26-2000||05:11 PM||||Man your pullulating the water that I drink and I fish in. <P>No seriously, removing the converter usually causes you to loose low-end torque while you gain less top end power than the torque you lost. It’s not worth it and my water already tastes bad enough. You exhaust also gets louder without the converter. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Slammed Fiero||12-26-2000||05:45 PM||||yank the cat and get a chris west flowmaster muffler. Or swap the cat for a random Tech hi flow unit. Summit also sells a generic hi flow cat , this is what Chriss moore has on his car lisa.<P>Chris west's muffler is $140.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...||||reg||1
Z||000010||BAM-BAM||12-26-2000||06:25 PM||||  You have to wonder about the validity of the catalitic converter in the first place. When I originaly smogged my 85GT with the cat it passed. Later, after having some emissions problems, and the cat being suspect per the dealer, a friend of mine hollowed it out for me (they look here) and it passed even cleaner than before. Question; in states where they don't do a visual inspection and vehicals without cats pass, what use is the cat in the first place?<BR> Seems to be one of those federal must haves that really do nothing but appease the folks that are told that they do. I think I'll go with Rodneys cat eliminator and split the case on my cat and weld it on around the strait pipe. Don't tell anyone, SHHH!<P> Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach/KYB Susp.<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats,headliner! MOMO Commando Steering Wheel, White Face Race Style Gauges And Soon To Be More!!! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||12
Z||000011||Will||12-27-2000||09:48 AM||||A good thing to do with your exhaust would be to put a 3.4 between ported stock headers  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||william_thorniii||12-27-2000||10:04 AM||||FL,<P>I have been an OH resident all of my life.  I do own a "classic" car... a 1956 Ford Fairlane.  Needless to say I do not need to pass the sniffer test with this car.  <P>The last I understood it; however, a car was required to be 25 years new before it could be considered "classic".  This 25 year requirement is the same age required to pass Ohio e-check for the 17 counties lucky enough to have the testing (of which I live).  Please fill me in on how your cars (the same age as mine) became classified as "classic" vehicles and thereby not required to pass Ohio e-check.  I was aware that if ANY vehicle was driven less than 3000k per year it could be then classified as "classic" but I have some questions about this approach.  <P>Many Thanks,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000013||WKDFIRO||12-27-2000||03:42 PM||||With the exhaust questions I have to post what I have done with mine and what I have experienced with my 85 GT.<P>Many in my club have gone to 2.5 inch exhuast with Flowmaster and have been GREATLY pleased with the results. We have 3.4s with Cats and without cats, some with stock exhaust and some with 2.5 inch exhaust. Keith "Fiero Doc" Huff has a tricked 3.4 with 2.5 inch exhaust with flanged cat (so he can put it on or take it off) going through a flowmaster and Z28 Camaro exhaust tips. Sounds GREAT! Without the cat, the sound is much more...authoritative. I listened to one Fiero with a 3.2 bore and stroke with straight pipes all the way to the tips and thought the snarling note was just to die for! <P>So I decided to try something I hadn't heard anyone else do. I went 2.5 inch mandrel bent straight pipe from the cat to 4.5 inch Megs tips with baffles (no muffler). I figured that the cat would give me just enough with the baffles to keep me sounding good.<P>When I got the car from the exhaust shop in Orange Co. (Welding Works, great place!) the owner told me that it was going to be loud. When I started her up it was like starting up a beast! I got out with a big grin on my face and thanked him for his masterful work. I got out and drove it around and around 3000 rpm a low deep resonance was filling the cabin making my fillings vibrate. I drove back and asked him what could be done with it. He asked me if I knew that the cat was blown out. It was hollow, all blown out years ago it looked like. He could either put a cat in or a glass pack to quiet it down inside because the cat is now acting as an expansion chamber. <P>Im still getting compliments on it, the cam sounds really good from the pipes but sometimes on the freeway I can't hear myself think! I'll be going back soon for the Cat and let you all know how that goes. I think that the cat will give me just enough back preasure and sound control. Otherwise its runs great! A nice improvement!<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 12-27-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000014||Gonzo_442||12-27-2000||05:38 PM||||William,<P>check into it, but in PA i believe the requirement is 25 years old OR no longer produced. the fiero would qualify under the second option, but classic cars are also limited in mileage per year, etc. meaning you couldn't use it for a daily driver.<P>--<BR>g||||reg||1
Z||000015||FieroLisa||12-27-2000||06:09 PM||||William,<P>The difference is in classic and collectors. I'm not sure on the factors of it being a collector, except my 86 coupe was one, and therefor I have no doubt that the GT is one too. Probably something to do with the fact that it's not made anymore. What I do know is, call the license bureau, request the plate, tell them your VIN and they will check that it can get the plate. There is a $40 fee I think...either $30 or $40, all I know is I payed $70 for personalized collectors plates. There is a mileage restraint, but since my cars are only summer drivers, I don't every worry about it. Something like you can drive it 2 days a week, or 3, I don;t know, I got the plates a year or so ago.  I also believe the yearly renewal fee is higher, but then again you get your sticker without anything to do with e-check. Just call and you will find out all the answers. Soon as I get back to Ohio in January, I'm getting personalized collectors for the GT too...<P><P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>86 gold 5 speed SC (For Sale)<BR>Columbus, OH/Greensboro NC<BR>*Lots of New Pics!* <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000016||the need for speed||12-27-2000||09:59 PM||||what about putting on a true duel exhaust, wouldn't this be a good thing like a corrvet.and if it is not a good idea why fake duel exhaust?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Hank is Here||12-27-2000||10:28 PM||||The way it works in Pa for classic/antique tags is...<BR>classic- over 15 years old, don't know about inspection, registeration, or milage  <BR>antique-over 25 years old, one lifetime registeration(sp)($72), no inspection-safety or emissions, and 2500 miles max a year.  I have two antique tags and like them.<P>Mark Johnson   ||||reg||1
Z||000018||WKDFIRO||12-28-2000||05:14 AM||||True dual exhaust is not easy in the Fiero. Plus the V6 gives off uneven pulses which can give it an odd idle sound. Having a crossover somewhere helps with flow. V8s have been dynoed with better flow and better hp/torque with a crossover at some point as opposed to two singular pipes. Even the new Vettes (I understand) have a crossover between the exhaust pipes.<P> <p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 12-28-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000019||Philphine||12-28-2000||10:47 AM||||behind the curve i guess. i just have the 4 cyl. with a gt exhaust and free flow cat. i told the shop that i want to try a bigger engine one day and he said this would do for any thing i wanted to put in. not sure if he know whats possibe to install in it.<P>doing slow work on putting chambered sidepipes on my chevy and thinking one might be good for a single outlet fiero exhaust. i think they make dual outlet ones too but the bends to get it in a fiero might be detrimental to whatever benifits you'd gain.<P>just occured to me that i guess you might be able to get two chambered pipes in for duals since they're small diameter wise. <p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 12-28-2000).]||||reg||1
